WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.060
A dangling pointer refers to an object or storage that is no longer valid.

2
00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:14.040
This can happen when code returns the address of a local object, stores access to a temporary, keeps a pointer after deletion, or saves a view into data that later goes away.

3
00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:19.740
The pointer value may still look like an address, but the program no longer has a valid object behind it.

4
00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:25.140
Lifetime hazards often come from hidden relationships rather than one dramatic mistake.

5
00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:27.880
A helper may store a pointer that was meant only for the call.

6
00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:31.420
A callback may run after the owner has been destroyed.

7
00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:36.560
A container may reallocate and invalidate pointers into its elements.

8
00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:40.580
Code may move an object and then rely on details of its old state.

9
00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:45.320
Each case is a contract problem: access lived longer than the state it described.

10
00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:48.800
Pointer escape is a key review concept.

11
00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:57.700
Whenever a pointer crosses a boundary, ask whether it is used immediately, stored, returned, captured, or passed to asynchronous work.

12
00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:00.760
Short-lived borrows are easier to verify.

13
00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:05.260
Long-lived access requires a stronger ownership or observation model.

14
00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:12.640
If the pointer may be stored, the API should say who guarantees the pointee remains alive and how invalidation is handled.

15
00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:16.120
Prevention relies on ownership clarity and RAII.

16
00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:21.500
Return values or owning handles when lifetime would otherwise be ambiguous.

17
00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:26.000
Use containers and smart pointers to tie cleanup to object lifetime.

18
00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:28.740
Keep views shorter-lived than their source data.

19
00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:38.040
Also review exceptional exits and early returns: cleanup may happen sooner than the caller expects, and stored pointers may outlive partially completed initialization.

20
00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:47.580
Test teardown, cancellation, movement, and container modification paths because those are where dangling access often becomes visible.
